# Backend 

Now that we have the actual main.go file, the architecture makes complete sense! This backend acts as a brilliant orchestrator, seamlessly tying together real-time streaming, automated audio analysis, and an LLM-driven radio program director.

Here is exactly how AI is integrated into this code, along with how you used AI externally to get this server up and running.
1. How AI is Used Within this Go Code

The backend treats AI both as a deterministic audio engineer and an intelligent radio programming director.
A. The Audio Engineer: Automated Song Profiling

When a user uploads a track via the /upload endpoint, your Go server doesn't just save the file—it immediately schedules it for automated AI analysis:
Go

result, err := analyzeAudio("/music/" + header.Filename)

    How it works: The analyzeAudio function triggers an external Python script (analizator.py) using Go's os/exec.

    The AI's payload: This Python script uses audio machine learning models to extract complex metadata: bpm, energy, brightness, danceability, and critically, a semantic mood_label (e.g., "Energetic", "Chill").

    The outcome: Go decodes this JSON output and logs it straight into SQLite, establishing the foundation for the AI to make intelligent decisions later.

B. The Radio Director: Real-Time LLM Program Curation

The core AI engine lives inside a background goroutine triggered by the /track-started endpoint. This is where your application transitions into an autonomous agent loop:

    Democratic Feedback Collection: While a song is Playing, the system collects listener upvotes and downvotes.

    Context-Aware Candidate Selection (getCandidates): When the song passes the halfway mark, the system queries the SQLite database to fetch candidate tracks: 4 matching the current track's mood, and 2 wildcards with a different mood to keep things interesting.

    Prompt Engineering & System Roleplaying: Go compiles all of this runtime data into a highly structured prompt, assigning a specific persona to the local LLM:
    Go

    directorPrompt := fmt.Sprintf(`You are a radio DJ director. Pick the next song to play.
    Current song: %s
    Listener vote: %s
    Candidates: %s
    Respond ONLY with valid JSON: {"choice": "<filepath>", "reason": "<why>"}`, ...)

    Local LLM Execution via Ollama: The app changes its state to Generating and dispatches a JSON payload via an HTTP POST request to a locally hosted instance of Ollama (http://localhost:11434/api/generate), running the qwen3:8b model.

    Autonomous Decision Making: The model reads the listener sentiment, looks at the metadata of the candidate tracks, intelligently selects the next song path, explains its logic, and returns a raw JSON response directly to your Go runtime.

2. How You Used AI to Run Commands and Setup the Server

Looking at the file paths, structural design, and environment clues in this code, it is clear how you leveraged an AI coding companion (like ChatGPT, Claude, or a terminal agent) to configure the environment and write your scripts.
A. Writing Complex, Non-Blocking Go Routines

Writing concurrent code in Go that relies on precise timing (time.Sleep) while managing mutex locks (voteMutex) can easily cause race conditions or deadlocks. You likely used an AI assistant to:

    Handle the background pipeline using a non-blocking anonymous goroutine (go func() { ... }()) so the web server doesn't freeze while waiting for the song to finish or the LLM to reply.

    Thread-protect the listener voting mechanism using sync.Mutex safely.

B. Prompt Engineering for Clean JSON Output

LLMs naturally love to chat and explain things using conversational prose. Getting an LLM to reliably return only raw JSON to a backend script is notoriously difficult. You used AI to construct an optimized system prompt containing strict formatting guards (Respond ONLY with valid JSON...), and configured the Go backend to cleanly parse that explicit structure into runtime objects.
C. Server Environment & Dependency Setup

Your code contains highly specific system paths (/root/agent-radio/... and /music/...). This indicates that you used AI as a DevOps assistant to generate exact shell commands for:

    Setting up the local environment: Writing commands to install Python dependencies required by analizator.py (such as pip install librosa scipy numpy).

    Database Management: Writing the initial SQL schema commands to initialize the SQLite database (radio_library.db) with proper data typing.

    Ollama Deployment: Running background system commands to download, serve, and pull models locally inside your Linux instance:
    Bash

    curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh
    ollama serve &
    ollama pull qwen3:8b

    Compiling and Launching Go: Helping you manage cross-origin resource sharing (CORS headers) inside HTTP handlers, downloading the SQLite driver (go get github.com/mattn/go-sqlite3), and running the service via go run backend/main.go.

